[
{
"content_id": "23043",
"reference_id": 22211,
"version": "1.0.4",
"title": "Obadiah 1:1",
"media_type": "Text",
"index_reference": "31001001",
"language": "eng",
"review_level": "Professional",
"content": "<ul><li><p>The word <em>vision</em> introduces a prophetic book (<data class=\"bible-ref\" data-start-ref=\"23001001\" data-end-ref=\"23001001\">Isaiah 1:1</data>; <data class=\"bible-ref\" data-start-ref=\"34001001\" data-end-ref=\"34001001\">Nahum 1:1</data>). It also describes what a prophet saw or sensed as a divine message (<data class=\"bible-ref\" data-start-ref=\"26012026\" data-end-ref=\"26012027\">Ezekiel 12:26–27</data>; <data class=\"bible-ref\" data-start-ref=\"27008001\" data-end-ref=\"27008001\">Daniel 8:1</data>). A vision not from the Lord was false (<data class=\"bible-ref\" data-start-ref=\"24014014\" data-end-ref=\"24014014\">Jeremiah 14:14</data>; <data class=\"bible-ref\" data-start-ref=\"24023016\" data-end-ref=\"24023016\">23:16</data>), proven by its failure to happen (<data class=\"bible-ref\" data-start-ref=\"26012022\" data-end-ref=\"26012024\">Ezekiel 12:22–24</data>). In contrast, true visions of events, whether near or far, do happen (<data class=\"bible-ref\" data-start-ref=\"26012025\" data-end-ref=\"26012025\">Ezekiel 12:25</data>; <data class=\"bible-ref\" data-start-ref=\"35002003\" data-end-ref=\"35002003\">Habakkuk 2:3</data>). Prophetic visions appeared in different forms, like dreams or extrasensory experiences (for example, <data class=\"bible-ref\" data-start-ref=\"27008002\" data-end-ref=\"27008002\">Daniel 8:2</data>). The lack of visions shows divine abandonment (<data class=\"bible-ref\" data-start-ref=\"25002009\" data-end-ref=\"25002009\">Lamentations 2:9</data>; <data class=\"bible-ref\" data-start-ref=\"26007026\" data-end-ref=\"26007026\">Ezekiel 7:26</data>; <data class=\"bible-ref\" data-start-ref=\"33003006\" data-end-ref=\"33003006\">Micah 3:6</data>). </p></li><li><p>Obadiah was a common Hebrew name meaning “servant of the Lord.” The prophet Obadiah is unknown outside this book. </p></li><li><p><span><em>an envoy has been sent among the nations</em></span>: In ancient warfare, allies were contacted to join a military group to punish an enemy or defend against an attack. Here, the Sovereign Lord calls the nations to address Edom’s evil pride. </p></li><li><p><em>let us god to battle</em>: This is a willing group, but ironically, they will later be punished for their own evil actions (<data class=\"bible-ref\" data-start-ref=\"31001015\" data-end-ref=\"31001016\">Obadiah 1:15–16</data>).</p></li></ul>",
