"content_id": "23051",
"reference_id": 22219,
"version": "1.0.4",
"title": "Obadiah 1:8",
"media_type": "Text",
"index_reference": "31001008",
"language": "eng",
"review_level": "Professional",
"content": "<ul><li><p><em>In that day</em>: The Hebrew word <em>yom</em>, meaning “day,” appears 12 times in <data class=\"bible-ref\" data-start-ref=\"31001008\" data-end-ref=\"31001015\">Obadiah 1:8–15</data>. It is often translated as “when” or “at that time.” The time mentioned in <data class=\"bible-ref\" data-start-ref=\"31001008\" data-end-ref=\"31001008\">Obadiah 1:8</data> marks the start of the end, when God intervenes to change the fate of his people. </p></li><li><p><span><em>wise men … men of understanding</em></span>: Edom was proud of its wisdom tradition (see <data class=\"bible-ref\" data-start-ref=\"24049007\" data-end-ref=\"24049007\">Jeremiah 49:7</data>). For instance, the book of Job takes place in Edomite land.</p></li></ul>",
