{
"content_id": "23034",
"reference_id": 22202,
"version": "1.0.4",
"title": "Obadiah 1:11",
"media_type": "Text",
"index_reference": "31001011",
"language": "eng",
"review_level": "Professional",
"content": "<p>Outside of this book, we know little about Edom&apos;s role during the Babylonian invasions of Judah in 605, 597, and 586 BC. God&apos;s law requires responsibility toward one&apos;s neighbor (for example, <data class=\"bible-ref\" data-start-ref=\"05022001\" data-end-ref=\"05022004\">Deuteronomy 22:1–4</data>; compare <data class=\"bible-ref\" data-start-ref=\"40025041\" data-end-ref=\"40025046\">Matthew 25:41–46</data>), especially when there is a specific <span><em>covenant of brotherhood</em></span> (<data class=\"bible-ref\" data-start-ref=\"30001009\" data-end-ref=\"30001009\">Amos 1:9</data>) or a historical relationship which biblical writers considered a relationship between brothers (see <data class=\"resource-ref\" data-content-id=\"22217\" data-resource-code=\"AquiferOpenStudyNotes\">study note on Obadiah 1:6</data>).</p>",
"associations": {
